ated Alignment Evaluation for Autoformalization\")\n                                8.  [G Comparison with Human Evaluation and LLM-as-judge](#A7 \"In 6 Conclusion ‣ 5.3 Effects of Different Alignment Score 𝒱_\\\"align\\\" ‣ 5 Analysis and Discussion ‣ 4.4 Comparison with Human Evaluation and LLM-As-Judge ‣ 4.3 Main Results ‣ 4.2 Metrics ‣ 4.1 Datasets ‣ 4 Experiment ‣ FormalAlign: Automated Alignment Evaluation for Autoformalization\")\n                                    1.  [Experiment Design](#A7.SS0.SSS0.Px1 \"In Appendix G Comparison with Human Evaluation and LLM-as-judge ‣ 6 Conclusion ‣ 5.3 Effects of Different Alignment Score 𝒱_\\\"align\\\" ‣ 5 Analysis and Discussion ‣ 4.4 Comparison with Human Evaluation and LLM-As-Judge ‣ 4.3 Main Results ‣ 4.2 Metrics ‣ 4.1 Datasets ‣ 4 Experiment ‣ FormalAlign: Automated Alignment Evaluation for Autoformalization\")\n                                    2.  [Results](#A7.SS0.SSS0.Px2 \"In Appendix G Comparison with Human Evaluation and LLM-as-judge ‣ 6 Conclusion ‣ 5.3 Effects of Different Alignment Score 𝒱_\\\"align\\\" ‣ 5 Analysis and Discussion ‣ 4.4 Comparison with Human Evaluation and LLM-As-Judge ‣ 4.3 Main Results ‣ 4.2 Metrics ‣ 4.1 Datasets ‣ 4 Experiment ‣ FormalAlign: Automated Alignment Evaluation for Autoformalization\")\n                                9.  [H Case Study](#A8 \"In 6 Conclusion ‣ 5.3 Effects of Different Alignment Score 𝒱_\\\"align\\\" ‣ 5 Analysis and Discussion ‣ 4.4 Comparison with Human Evaluation and LLM-As-Judge ‣ 4.3 Main Results ‣ 4.2 Metrics ‣ 4.1 Datasets ‣ 4 Experiment ‣ FormalAlign: Automated Alignment Evaluation for Autoformalization\")\n\n[License: CC BY 4.0](https://info.arxiv.org/help/license/index.html#licenses-available)\n\narXiv:2410.10135v1 \\[cs.CL\\] 14 Oct 2024\n\n1 ππ\\\\pi1 ⊢⟹\\\\Longrightarrow1\n\n# FormalAlign: Automated Alignment  \nEvaluation for Autoformalization\n\nJianqiao Lu ††thanks: Leading co-authors with equal contribution.    Yingjia Wan, Yinya Huang, Jing Xiong, Zhengying Liu, Zhijiang Guo ††thanks: Corresponding Author. Affiliation: Huawei Noah’s Ark Lab City University of Hong Kongjqlu@cs.hku.hk, {yingjiawan.alisa, cartusguo}@gmail.com Affiliation: The University of Hong Kong University of Cambridge\n\n###### Abstract\n\nAutoformalization aims to convert informal mathematical proofs into machine-verifiable formats, bridging the gap between natural and formal languages. However, ensuring semantic alignment between the informal and formalized statements remains challenging. Existing approaches heavily rely on manual verification, hindering scalability. To address this, we introduce FormalAlign, the first automated framework designed for evaluating the alignment between natural and formal languages in autoformalization. FormalAlign trains on both the autoformalization sequence generation task and the representational alignment between input and output, employing a dual loss that combines a pair of mutually enhancing autoformalization and alignment tasks. Evaluated across four benchmarks augmented by our proposed misalignment strategies, FormalAlign demonstrates superior performance. In our experiments, FormalAlign outperforms GPT-4, achieving an Alignment-Selection Score 11.58% higher on FormL4-Basic (99.21% vs. 88.91%) and 3.19% higher on MiniF2F-Valid (66.39% vs. 64.34%). This effective alignment evaluation significantly reduces the need for manual verification. Both the dataset and code can be accessed via [https://github.com/rookie-joe/FormalAlign](https://github.com/rookie-joe/FormalAlign).\n\n## 1 Introduction\n\nAutoformalization is the task of automatically converting informal theorems and proofs into machine-verifiable formats ([Wang et al., 2018](#bib.bib45); [Szegedy, 2020](#bib.bib42); [Wu et al., 2022](#bib.bib49); [Jiang et al., 2023c](#bib.bib20)). It bridges the gap between natural and formal languages, leveraging the strengths of both: natural language carries extensive logical reasoning and human knowledge. while formal language enables rigorous verification and proof ([Kaliszyk et al., 2014](#bib.bib21)). While promising, autoformalization faces challenges in ensuring semantic alignment between these languages. The availability of fully formalized and computer-checked content is limited ([Kaliszyk et al., 2017](#bib.bib22)). This lack of alignment information hinders the development of robust autoformalization models ([Bansal & Szegedy, 2020](#bib.bib4)).\n\n![Refer to caption](2410.10135v1/model_example_horizontal.png)\n\nFigure 1: A comparison of current methods and FormalAlign in evaluating autoformalization. The formal statement is misaligned with the natural language statement: it incorrectly ends with 80∗c\\=6380\\*c=63, when the aligned equation should be 63∗a\\=80∗c63\\*a=80\\*c. Current methods can only verify the surface-form integrity of the autoformalized sequence via BLEU or by passing it to a formal language compiler, while our FormalAlign successfully detects the semantic misalignment of the autoformalized statement with the informal sequence.\n\nCurrent evaluation for autoformalization ([Jiang et al., 2023c](#bib.bib20); [Huang et al., 2024](#bib.bib16)) focus solely on logical validity, which can be easily verified by formal language compilers (e.g., the Lean 4 compiler11 1 Details of the compiler are provided in Appendix [A](#A1 \"Appendix A Lean 4 Compiler ‣ 6 Conclusion ‣ 5.3 Effects of Different Alignment Score 𝒱_\\\"align\\\" ‣ 5 Analysis and Discussion ‣ 4.4 Comparison with Human Evaluation and LLM-As-Judge ‣ 4.3 Main Results ‣ 4.2 Metrics ‣ 4.1 Datasets ‣ 4 Experiment ‣ FormalAlign: Automated Alignment Evaluation for Autoformalization\").). Another direct but suboptimal evaluation resort to surface form matching via BLEU ([Papineni et al., 2002](#bib.bib36)), which is widely used by recent works ([Wu et al., 2022](#bib.bib49); [Jiang et al., 2023c](#bib.bib20); [Azerbayev et al., 2023a](#bib.bib2)), but struggles with semantic alignment or logical equivalence ([Li et al., 2024](#bib.bib25)).\n\nTake the case in Figure [1](#S1.F1 \"Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ FormalAlign: Automated Alignment Evaluation for Autoformalization\") as an example, to correctly translate the natural language proof target into a Lean 4 statement, first, the variables for the objects “ligs”, “lags”, and “lugs” should be included and real numbers greater than zero. Then, the two equations should be translated into two corresponding hypotheses h1h\\_{1} and h2h\\_{2}. Finally, the proof target “How many ligs are equivalent to 80 lugs? Show that 63” needs to be formalized into “63∗a\\=80∗c63\\*a=80\\*c”, which is failed in this case by omitting the pronounced “ligs”. However, because the incorrectly translated “80∗c\\=6380\\*c=63” is logically valid in Lean 4 and similar to the ground truth in surface form, it is flawless to a theorem compiler or the BLEU score. The semantic misalignment of lacking a “lig” in the equation is undetected. Moreover, due to its elusive nature, this misalignment is often challenging to detect, even with methods like BERTscore ([Zhang et al., 2020b](#bib.bib58)), which are designed to assess semantic similarity, Therefore, a robust and effective approach to Automated Alignment Evaluation (AAE) is urgently needed.\n\nTo bridge this gap, we introduce the FormalAlign framework, which assesses the alignment between informal and formal languages during autoformalization. As demonstrated in Figure [2](#S2.F2 \"Figure 2 ‣ Evaluation for Generation ‣ 2 Related Work ‣ FormalAlign: Automated Alignment Evaluation for Autoformalization\"), FormalAlign learns both the sequence generation task of autoformalization (top half in Figure [2](#S2.F2 \"Figure 2 ‣ Evaluation for Generation ‣ 2 Related Work ‣ FormalAlign: Automated Alignment Evaluation for Autoformalization\")) and the representational alignment (bottom half in Figure [2](#S2.F2 \"Figure 2 ‣ Evaluation for Generation ‣ 2 Related Work ‣ FormalAlign: Automated Alignment Evaluation for Autoformalization\")) between input and output. FormalAlign jointly trains the pair of mutually enhancing tasks. This encourages the model to generate similar embeddings for corresponding pairs and distinct embeddings for non-corresponding pairs, enhancing its ability to differentiate between aligned and misaligned sequences, as the case in Figure [1](#S1.F1 \"Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ FormalAlign: Automated Alignment Evaluation for Autoformalization\").\n\nWe evaluate FormalAlign on four benchmarks sourced from MiniF2F ([Zheng et al., 2022b](#bib.bib62)) and FormL4 ([Lu et al., 2024b](#bib.bib32)). Compared with GPT-4, FormalAlign achieves a substantially higher precision score across these datasets, e.g., in the FormL4-Basic dataset (93.65% vs. 26.33%). It also outperforms GPT-4 in alignment-selection score across multiple datasets, including a remarkable 99.21% vs. 88.91% in FormL4-Basic and 66.39% vs. 64.34% in MiniF2F-Valid. Extensive experimental results demonstrate the effectiveness of FormalAlign, significantly reducing the reliance on manual verification. Our contributions are summarized as follows:\n\n-   •\n    \n    To the best of our knowledge, we design the first method for automatically evaluating alignment in autoformalization, reducing the reliance for manual verification.\n    \n-   •\n    \n    We develop a combined loss framework that simultaneously enhances a model for both autoformalization and semantic alignment.\n    \n-   •\n    \n    Extensive experiments on established autoformalization benchmarks demonstrate the effectiveness and robustness of FormalAlign.\n    \n\n## 2 Related Work\n\n#### Autoformalization with LLMs\n\nEarly efforts ([Wang et al., 2018](#bib.bib45); [Bansal & Szegedy, 2020](#bib.bib4)) employed encoder-decoder neural networks to translate informal statements into formal languages like Mizar ([Rudnicki, 1992](#bib.bib38)), HOL Light ([Harrison, 1996](#bib.bib15)), and Coq ([Barras et al., 1997](#bib.bib5)). The advent of LLMs ([Chen et al., 2021](#bib.bib9); [Chowdhery et al., 2022](#bib.bib11); [Lewkowycz et al., 2022](#bib.bib24); [Achiam et al., 2023](#bib.bib1)) enhances the capabilities of autoformalization ([Wu et al., 2022](#bib.bib49); [Jiang et al., 2023a](#bib.bib18)). Some approaches directly prompt LLMs ([Wu et al., 2022](#bib.bib49); [Jiang et al., 2023c](#bib.bib20); [Zhao et al., 2023](#bib.bib60); [Jiang et al., 2023a](#bib.bib18)) to translate mathematical problems into formal languages like Isabelle ([Wenzel et al., 2008](#bib.bib48)) and Lean ([de Moura et al., 2015](#bib.bib13)). On the other hand, training or fine-tuning LLMs with paired formal-informal data ([Azerbayev et al., 2023b](#bib.bib3); [Ying et al., 2024](#bib.bib53); [Shao et al., 2024b](#bib.bib41); [Lu et al., 2024b](#bib.bib32)) garner increasing attention for its effectiveness in enhancing LLMs’ performance in autoformalization. The evaluation of autoformalization primarily depends on manually verifying the alignment between informal and formalized statements ([Li et al., 2024](#bib.bib25)). There is a pressing need for an efficient and less labor-intensive method for automated autoformalization alignment.\n\n#### Evaluation for Generation\n\nThe challenges of automated evaluating natural language generation tasks, grow as the difficulty of tasks increases. N-gram-based metrics ([Papineni et al., 2002](#bib.bib36); [Lin, 2004](#bib.bib26)) resort to surface-form matching, which has been beneficial for evaluation tasks with static references such as image-captioning ([Young et al., 2014](#bib.bib54); [Chen et al., 2015](#bib.bib10)) and text summarization ([See et al., 2017](#bib.bib39); [Narayan et al., 2018](#bib.bib34)). Semantics are rarely considered until embedding-based metrics emerge, especially metrics leveraging the evolving pre-trained language models ([Zhang et al., 2020a](#bib.bib57); [Yuan et al., 2021](#bib.bib55); [Qin et al., 2023](#bib.bib37)) and LLMs ([Xu et al., 2023](#bib.bib51); [Liu et al., 2023c](#bib.bib29); [Liu et al., 2024](#bib.bib30)). The growth of LLMs continues empowering parameter-based metrics for advanced evaluation such as multi-agent ([Chan et al., 2023](#bib.bib7)), multi-aspect ([Liu et al., 2023b](#bib.bib28)) and multi-proxies ([Tan et al., 2024](#bib.bib43)) The other line of work fine-tunes language models for scoring ([Ke et al., 2023](#bib.bib23); [Wang et al., 2023](#bib.bib46); [Yue et al., 2023](#bib.bib56)), labeling ([Gekhman et al., 2023](#bib.bib14)), text probability calculation ([Liu et al., 2023b](#bib.bib28)), or comparison ([Wang et al., 2023](#bib.bib46); [Zheng et al., 2023](#bib.bib63)) to enhance and adjust for evaluation targets. In this paper, we propose an automated evaluator for the challenging yet under-explored autoformalization evaluation that requires both rigorous logical validity and aligned semantics between the natural-formal pair. To this end, we fine-tune LLMs via joint autoformalization generation and representational alignment tasks and obtain a logically and semantically empowered aligner.\n\n![Refer to caption](2410.10135v1/main.png)\n\nFigure 2: An overview of FormalAlign, which combines the cross-entropy loss in sequence autoformalization and the contrastive loss in hidden states to enhance the informal-formal alignment.\n\n## 3 Method: FormalAlign\n\nThe FormalAlign framework trains an LLM that can evaluate the alignment between natural (informal) and formal languages during autoformalization. As illustrated in Figure [2](#S2.F2 \"Figure 2 ‣ Evaluation for Generation ‣ 2 Related Work ‣ FormalAlign: Automated Alignment Evaluation for Autoformalization\"), FormalAlign combines two types of loss in the training process: one for the sequence generation task of autoformalization and another for the representational alignment between input and output. This dual loss framework mutually enhances autoformalization and alignment.\n\n### 3.1 Notations\n\nWe first define the notations as follows:\n\n𝐍𝐋i\\\\mathbf{NL}\\_{i} : The it​hi^{th} informal input sequence in a batch, 𝐍𝐋i\\=(𝐍𝐋i,1,𝐍𝐋i,2,…,𝐍𝐋i,m)\\\\mathbf{NL}\\_{i}=(\\\\mathbf{NL}\\_{i,1},\\\\mathbf{NL}\\_{i,2},\\\\ldots,\\\\mathbf{NL}\\_{i,m}) , where mm is the sequence length of 𝐍𝐋i\\\\mathbf{NL}\\_{i} .\n\n𝐅𝐋i\\\\mathbf{FL}\\_{i} : The it​hi^{th} ground-truth formal output sequence in a batch, 𝐅𝐋i\\=(𝐅𝐋i,1,𝐅𝐋i,2,…,𝐅𝐋i,n)\\\\mathbf{FL}\\_{i}=(\\\\mathbf{FL}\\_{i,1},\\\\mathbf{FL}\\_{i,2},\\\\ldots,\\\\mathbf{FL}\\_{i,n}) , where nn is the sequence length of 𝐅𝐋i\\\\mathbf{FL}\\_{i} .\n\nPϕ​(𝐅𝐋i,j|𝐅𝐋i,<j,𝐍𝐋i)P\\_{\\\\phi}(\\\\mathbf{FL}\\_{i,j}|\\\\mathbf{FL}\\_{i,<j},\\\\mathbf{NL}\\_{i}) : The probability of predicting the jt​hj^{th} token in the formal sequence 𝐅𝐋i\\\\mathbf{FL}\\_{i} by the auto-regressive language model with parameters ϕ\\\\phi , given the previous tokens 𝐅𝐋i,<j\\\\mathbf{FL}\\_{i,<j} in the formal sequence and the informal input 𝐍𝐋i\\\\mathbf{NL}\\_{i} .\n\nZϕ​(𝐍𝐋i)Z\\_{\\\\phi}(\\\\mathbf{NL}\\_{i}) : The hidden state from the auto-regressive language model with parameters ϕ\\\\phi for the final position in the it​hi^{th} informal input 𝐍𝐋i\\\\mathbf{NL}\\_{i} , i.e., 𝐍𝐋i,m\\\\mathbf{NL}\\_{i,m} .\n\nZϕ​(𝐅𝐋i|𝐍𝐋i)Z\\_{\\\\phi}(\\\\mathbf{FL}\\_{i}|\\\\mathbf{NL}\\_{i}) : The hidden state from the auto-regressive language model with parameters ϕ\\\\phi for the final position in the it​hi^{th} ground-truth formal output 𝐅𝐋i\\\\mathbf{FL}\\_{i} , i.e., 𝐅𝐋i,n\\\\mathbf{FL}\\_{i,n} , conditioned on the paired it​hi^{th} informal input 𝐍𝐋i\\\\mathbf{NL}\\_{i} .\n\nZϕ​(𝐅𝐋i′|𝐍𝐋i)Z\\_{\\\\phi}(\\\\mathbf{FL}\\_{i^{\\\\prime}}|\\\\mathbf{NL}\\_{i}) : The hidden state from the auto-regressive language model with parameters ϕ\\\\phi for the final position in the (i′)t​h(i^{\\\\prime})^{th} unpaired formal output 𝐅𝐋i′\\\\mathbf{FL}\\_{i^{\\\\prime}} in a batch, conditioned on the it​hi^{th} informal input 𝐍𝐋i\\\\mathbf{NL}\\_{i} .\n\ncos​(⋅,⋅)\\\\text{cos}(\\\\cdot,\\\\cdot) : The cosine similarity between embeddings, defined as cos​(x,y)\\=x⋅y‖x‖⋅‖y‖\\\\text{cos}(x,y)=\\\\frac{x\\\\cdot y}{\\\\|x\\\\|\\\\cdot\\\\|y\\\\|}.\n\nNN : The batch size.\n\n### 3.2 Training\n\n#### Autoformalization Task\n\nFor the autoformalization task of converting an informal input sequence 𝐍𝐋i\\\\mathbf{NL}\\_{i} to a formal output sequence 𝐅𝐋i\\\\mathbf{FL}\\_{i}, we use the cross-entropy loss function. This function measures the error in predicting each word in the formal sequence given the previous words and the informal input. It is defined as:\n\nℒC​E\\=−∑j\\=1nlogPϕ(𝐅𝐋i,j|𝐅𝐋i,j′|j′<j,𝐍𝐋i)\\\\displaystyle\\\\mathcal{L}\\_{CE}=-\\\\sum\\_{j=1}^{n}\\\\log P\\_{\\\\phi}(\\\\mathbf{FL}\\_{i,j}|\\\\mathbf{FL}\\_{i,j^{\\\\prime}|j^{\\\\prime}<j},\\\\mathbf{NL}\\_{i})\n\n#### Alignment Task\n\nTo ensure that the embeddings of the informal and formal sequences are well-aligned in the FormalAlign model, we introduce a contrastive loss ℒCL\\\\mathcal{L}\\_{\\\\text{CL}} . Let 𝐮i\\\\mathbf{u}\\_{i} and 𝐯i\\\\mathbf{v}\\_{i} denote the hidden state representations of the ii -th informal input 𝐍𝐋i\\\\mathbf{NL}\\_{i} and its corresponding formal output 𝐅𝐋i\\\\mathbf{FL}\\_{i} , respectively 𝐮i\\=Zϕ​(𝐍𝐋i)\\\\mathbf{u}\\_{i}=Z\\_{\\\\phi}(\\\\mathbf{NL}\\_{i}) and 𝐯i\\=Zϕ​(𝐅𝐋i|𝐍𝐋i)\\\\mathbf{v}\\_{i}=Z\\_{\\\\phi}(\\\\mathbf{FL}\\_{i}|\\\\mathbf{NL}\\_{i}).\n\nThe contrastive loss encourages the cosine similarity cos​(𝐮i,𝐯i)\\\\text{cos}(\\\\mathbf{u}\\_{i},\\\\mathbf{v}\\_{i}) between the representations of corresponding informal-formal pairs to be higher than the cosine similarity cos​(𝐮i,𝐯i′)\\\\text{cos}(\\\\mathbf{u}\\_{i},\\\\mathbf{v}\\_{i^{\\\\prime}}) between non-corresponding pairs:\n\nℒC​L\\=−1N∑i\\=1Nlogexp⁡(cos​(𝐮i,𝐯i)/τ)∑j\\=1Nexp⁡(cos​(𝐮i,𝐯j)/τ)\\\\displaystyle\\\\mathcal{L}\\_{CL}=-\\\\frac{1}{N}\\\\sum\\_{i=1}^{N}\\\\log\\\\frac{\\\\exp\\\\left({\\\\text{cos}(\\\\mathbf{u}\\_{i},\\\\mathbf{v}\\_{i})}/{\\\\tau}\\\\right)}{\\\\sum\\_{j=1}^{N}\\\\exp\\\\left({\\\\text{cos}(\\\\mathbf{u}\\_{i},\\\\mathbf{v}\\_{j})}/{\\\\tau}\\\\right)}\n\n(1)\n\nwhere τ\\\\tau is a temperature parameter that scales the cosine similarities. By minimizing this contrastive loss, the FormalAlign model learns to align the embeddings of corresponding informal-formal sequences while ensuring that the embeddings of non-corresponding sequences are dissimilar.\n\n#### FormalAlign Loss\n\nWe jointly train an evaluator model with the autoformalization and alignment tasks, resulting in a FormalAlign model. The combined training loss is:\n\nℒ\\=ℒC​E+ℒC​L\\\\displaystyle\\\\begin{aligned} \\\\mathcal{L}=\\\\mathcal{L}\\_{CE}+\\\\mathcal{L}\\_{CL}\\\\end{aligned}\n\n(2)\n\nWe train an alignment-aware FormalAlign model by minimizing a combined loss, enabling it to benefit from both the sequence alignment inherent in the autoformalization and the representation alignment facilitated by the contrastive learning process.\n\n### 3.3 Inference\n\nDuring the inference phase, the FormalAlign model generates an alignment evaluation score 𝒱align\\\\mathcal{V}\\_{\\\\text{align}} for each pair of informal input 𝐍𝐋i\\\\mathbf{NL}\\_{i} and formal output 𝐅𝐋i\\\\mathbf{FL}\\_{i} . This score combines two metrics: the certainty score and the similarity score.\n\n#### Certainty Score\n\nThe certainty score 𝒱cer\\\\mathcal{V}\\_{\\\\text{cer}} measures the confidence of the fine-tuned FormalAlign model in predicting the formal output based on the corresponding informal input. It is calculated by taking the exponential of the average log-probability assigned by the model to each token in the formal sequence:\n\n𝒱cer\\=exp⁡(1n​∑j\\=1nlog⁡Pϕ​(𝐅𝐋i,j|𝐅𝐋i,<j,𝐍𝐋i))\\\\displaystyle\\\\mathcal{V}\\_{\\\\text{cer}}=\\\\exp\\\\left(\\\\frac{1}{n}\\\\sum\\_{j=1}^{n}\\\\log P\\_{\\\\phi}(\\\\mathbf{FL}\\_{i,j}|\\\\mathbf{FL}\\_{i,<j},\\\\mathbf{NL}\\_{i})\\\\right)\n\n(3)\n\nwhere PϕP\\_{\\\\phi} represents the probability output of the model with parameters ϕ\\\\phi , 𝐅𝐋i,<j\\\\mathbf{FL}\\_{i,<j} denotes the tokens in the formal sequence up to position j−1j-1 , and nn is the length of the formal sequence.\n\n#### Similarity Score\n\nThe similarity score 𝒱sim\\\\mathcal{V}\\_{\\\\text{sim}} measures alignment between the embedding representations of the informal input and the formal output. It is computed using the cosine similarity between the hidden states of the informal input and the formal output conditioned on the informal input:\n\n𝒱sim\\=cos​(Zϕ​(𝐍𝐋i),Zϕ​(𝐅𝐋i|𝐍𝐋i))\\\\displaystyle\\\\mathcal{V}\\_{\\\\text{sim}}=\\\\text{cos}(Z\\_{\\\\phi}(\\\\mathbf{NL}\\_{i}),Z\\_{\\\\phi}(\\\\mathbf{FL}\\_{i}|\\\\mathbf{NL}\\_{i}))\n\n(4)\n\nwhere Zϕ​(𝐍𝐋i)Z\\_{\\\\phi}(\\\\mathbf{NL}\\_{i}) represents the hidden state from the final position in the informal input, and Zϕ​(𝐅𝐋i|𝐍𝐋i)Z\\_{\\\\phi}(\\\\mathbf{FL}\\_{i}|\\\\mathbf{NL}\\_{i}) represents the hidden state from the formal output conditioned on informal input.\n\n#### Alignment Score\n\nThe overall alignment evaluation score 𝒱align\\\\mathcal{V}\\_{\\\\text{align}} is computed by taking the average of the certainty score and the similarity score:\n\n𝒱align\\=(𝒱cer+𝒱sim)/2\\\\displaystyle\\\\mathcal{V}\\_{\\\\text{align}}={\\\\left(\\\\mathcal{V}\\_{\\\\text{cer}}+\\\\mathcal{V}\\_{\\\\text{sim}}\\\\right)}/{2}\n\n(5)\n\nThis combined score reflects both the accuracy of the translation from informal to formal expressions and the alignment of the internal representations of the sequences, providing a robust evaluation metric during the inference stage.\n\n## 4 Experiment\n\n### 4.1 Datasets\n\nIn our experimental setup, we conduct fine-tuning on the FormL4 ([Lu et al., 2024b](#bib.bib32)) and MMA ([Jiang et al., 2023a](#bib.bib18)) training sets, both of which are derived from Mathlib, a library of fundamental mathematical statements. This training data enables our model to align informal mathematical statements with their formal counterparts.\n\nWe employ a comprehensive set of test sets that covers both in-domain and out-of-domain data. Specifically, we use four distinct test sets: the basic and random test sets from FormL4, and the valid and test sets from MiniF2F ([Zheng et al., 2022a](#bib.bib61)). FormL4, designed to assess the autoformalization capabilities of LLMs in Lean 4 ([de Moura & Ullrich, 2021](#bib.bib12)) sourced from Mathlib, provides a comprehensive evaluation framework. The basic and random test sets from FormL4 allow us to gauge the model’s performance in autoformalizing fundamental math statements that are similar to the training data. In contrast, the validation and test sets from MiniF2F serve as out-of-domain test data, providing a more challenging evaluation setting. MiniF2F is a benchmark containing 488 manually formalized mathematical competition statements sourced from various mathematical olympiads (AMC, AIME, IMO) and high-school and undergraduate math classes. .\n\nThese datasets primarily provide paired input-output instances, lacking the negative examples crucial for a more robust assessment of our model. Consider one aligned informal-formal pair shown in [Section 4.1](#S4.SS1 \"4.1 Datasets ‣ 4 Experiment ‣ FormalAlign: Automated Alignment Evaluation for Autoformalization\") as an example. We detail our approach to generating misaligned formal outputs with the natural (informal) input employing strategies outlined in [Table 2](#S4.T2 \"In 4.1 Datasets ‣ 4 Experiment ‣ FormalAlign: Automated Alignment Evaluation for Autoformalization\"). The distribution of these misalignment types is visualized in [Figure 3](#S4.F3 \"In 4.1 Datasets ‣ 4 Experiment ‣ FormalAlign: Automated Alignment Evaluation for Autoformalization\").\n\nTable 1: Natural Language Statement and its aligned Lean Formal Statement.\n\nNatural Language Statement\n\nThe volume of a cone is given by the formula V\\=13​B​hV=\\\\frac{1}{3}Bh, where BB is the area of the base and hh is the height. The area of the base of a cone is 30 square units, and its height is 6.5 units. What is the number of cubic units in its volume? Show that it is 65.\n\nLean Formal Statement\n\n[⬇](data:text/plain;base64,dGhlb3JlbSBtYXRoZF9hbGdlYnJhXzQ3OAogIChiIGggdiA6IOKEnSkKICAoaOKCgCA6IDAgPCBiIOKIpyAwIDwgaCDiiKcgMCA8IHYpCiAgKGjigoEgOiB2ID0gMSAvIDMgKiAoYiAqIGgpKQogICho4oKCIDogYiA9IDMwKQogICho4oKDIDogaCA9IDEzIC8gMikgOgogIHYgPSA2NSA6PQ==) theorem mathd\\_algebra\\_478 (b h v : ℝ\\\\mathbb{R}) (h0 : 0 < b ∧\\\\wedge 0 < h ∧\\\\wedge 0 < v) (h1 : v \\= 1 / 3 \\* (b \\* h)) (h2 : b \\= 30) (h3 : h \\= 13 / 2) : v \\= 65 :=\n\nTable 2: Misalignment strategies.\n\nConstant Modification (constant)\n\nExponent Modification (exponent)\n\nThis type of misalignment involves changing a constant value within the expression. [⬇](data:text/plain;base64,IHRoZW9yZW0gbWF0aGRfYWxnZWJyYV80NzgKICAoYiBoIHYgOiDihJ0pCiAgKGhfMCA6IDAgPCBiIOKIpyAwIDwgaCDiiKcgMCA8IHYpCiAgKGhfMSA6IHYgPSAxIC8gMyAqIChiICogaCkpCiAgKGhfMiA6IGIgPSAzMSkgLS0gY2hhbmdlZCBjb25zdGFudAogIChoXzMgOiBoID0gMTMgLyAyKSA6CiAgdiA9IDY1IDo9) theorem mathd\\_algebra\\_478 (b h v : ℝ\\\\mathbb{R}) (h\\_0 : 0 < b ∧\\\\wedge 0 < h ∧\\\\wedge 0 < v) (h\\_1 : v \\= 1 / 3 \\* (b \\* h)) (h\\_2 : b \\= 31) \\-- changed constant (h\\_3 : h \\= 13 / 2) : v \\= 65 :=\n\nThis misalignment targets the exponents in the expression. [⬇](data:text/plain;base64,dGhlb3JlbSBtYXRoZF9hbGdlYnJhXzQ3OAogIChiIGggdiA6IOKEnSkKICAoaF8wIDogMCA8IGIg4oinIDAgPCBoIOKIpyAwIDwgdikKICAoaF8xIDogdiA9IDEgLyAzICogKGJeMiAqIGgpKSAtLSBjaGFuZ2VkIGV4cG9uZW50CiAgKGhfMiA6IGIgPSAzMCkKICAoaF8zIDogaCA9IDEzIC8gMikgOgogIHYgPSA2NSA6PQ==) theorem mathd\\_algebra\\_478 (b h v : ℝ\\\\mathbb{R}) (h\\_0 : 0 < b ∧\\\\wedge 0 < h ∧\\\\wedge 0 < v) (h\\_1 : v \\= 1 / 3 \\* (b^2 \\* h)) \\-- changed exponent (h\\_2 : b \\= 30) (h\\_3 : h \\= 13 / 2) : v \\= 65 :=\n\nIntroduction of a New Variable (variable\\_new)\n\nChange of Variable Type (variable\\_type)\n\nThis misalignment introduces a completely new variable into the expression. [⬇](data:text/plain;base64,dGhlb3JlbSBtYXRoZF9hbGdlYnJhXzQ3OAogIChiIGggdiB4IDog4oSdKSAtLSBhZGRlZCBhIG5ldyB2YXJpYWJsZSB4CiAgKGhfMCA6IDAgPCBiIOKIpyAwIDwgaCDiiKcgMCA8IHYpCiAgKGhfMSA6IHYgPSAxIC8gMyAqIChiICogaCkpCiAgKGhfMiA6IGIgPSAzMCkKICAoaF8zIDogaCA9IDEzIC8gMikgOgogIHYgPSA2NSA6PQ==) theorem mathd\\_algebra\\_478 (b h v x : ℝ\\\\mathbb{R}) \\-- added a new variable x (h\\_0 : 0 < b ∧\\\\wedge 0 < h ∧\\\\wedge 0 < v) (h\\_1 : v \\= 1 / 3 \\* (b \\* h)) (h\\_2 : b \\= 30) (h\\_3 : h \\= 13 / 2) : v \\= 65 :=\n\nIn this case, the misalignment involves changing the type of a variable within the expression. The function identifies the type of a randomly selected variable and changes it to a different type from a predefined list of types. [⬇](data:text/plain;base64,dGhlb3JlbSBtYXRoZF9hbGdlYnJhXzQ3OAogIChiIGggdiA6IOKEmikgLS0gY2hhbmdlZCB0eXBlIHRvIOKEmgogIChoXzAgOiAwIDwgYiDiiKcgMCA8IGgg4oinIDAgPCB2KQogIChoXzEgOiB2ID0gMSAvIDMgKiAoYiAqIGgpKQogIChoXzIgOiBiID0gMzApCiAgKGhfMyA6IGggPSAxMyAvIDIpIDoKICB2ID0gNjUgOj0=) theorem mathd\\_algebra\\_478 (b h v : ℚ\\\\mathbb{Q}) \\-- changed type to ℚ\\\\mathbb{Q} (h\\_0 : 0 < b ∧\\\\wedge 0 < h ∧\\\\wedge 0 < v) (h\\_1 : v \\= 1 / 3 \\* (b \\* h)) (h\\_2 : b \\= 30) (h\\_3 : h \\= 13 / 2) : v \\= 65 :=\n\nModification of Equality (equality)\n\nRandom Pairing (random)\n\nThis misalignment switches between equality \\=\\= and inequality ≠\\\\neq symbols within the expression. [⬇](data:text/plain;base64,dGhlb3JlbSBtYXRoZF9hbGdlYnJhXzQ3OAogIChiIGggdiA6IOKEnSkKICAoaF8wIDogMCA8IGIg4oinIDAgPCBoIOKIpyAwIDwgdikKICAoaF8xIDogdiDiiaAgMSAvIDMgKiAoYiAqIGgpKSAtLSBzd2FwcGVkIGluZXF1YWxpdHkKICAoaF8yIDogYiA9IDMwKQogIChoXzMgOiBoID0gMTMgLyAyKSA6CiAgdiA9IDY1IDo9) theorem mathd\\_algebra\\_478 (b h v : ℝ\\\\mathbb{R}) (h\\_0 : 0 < b ∧\\\\wedge 0 < h ∧\\\\wedge 0 < v) (h\\_1 : v ≠\\\\neq 1 / 3 \\* (b \\* h)) \\-- swapped inequality (h\\_2 : b \\= 30) (h\\_3 : h \\= 13 / 2) : v \\= 65 :=\n\nThis creates a mismatch between the informal input and its formal output. Instead of pairing the informal input with its correct formal output, this strategy randomly selects a formal output from other examples.\n\n![Refer to caption](2410.10135v1/figures/negative_examples.png)\n\nFigure 3: Distribution of misalignment types across datasets. This figure illustrates the variety and proportion of misalignment strategies applied to generate negative examples in the FormL4-Basic, FormL4-Random, MiniF2F-Valid, and MiniF2F-Test datasets.\n\n### 4.2 Metrics\n\nTo assess the performance of models in evaluating the alignment of informal and formal language pairs, we introduce three automated metrics:\n\nAlignment Selection (AS): This metric quantifies how well a model selects the aligned formal output from multiple candidates when given an informal input. We calculate the alignment evaluation score 𝒱align\\\\mathcal{V}\\_{\\\\text{align}} as described in [Section 3](#S3 \"3 Method: FormalAlign ‣ FormalAlign: Automated Alignment Evaluation for Autoformalization\") for each informal-formal pair. The pair with the highest score is selected as the aligned pair.\n\nAlignment Detection: We introduce a predefined threshold θ\\\\theta to detect the alignment for each informal-formal pair. If 𝒱align\\\\mathcal{V}\\_{\\\\text{align}} exceeds θ\\\\theta , the model considers the pair to be aligned. We evaluate this detection method using two metrics: precision and recall. Precision measures the fraction of pairs identified as aligned by the model that is truly informal-formal pairs. It is calculated as Precision\\=T​PT​P+F​P\\\\text{Precision}=\\\\frac{TP}{TP+FP}, where T​PTP represents the number of true positives (correctly identified aligned pairs) and F​PFP represents the number of false positives (incorrectly identified aligned pairs). Recall measures the fraction of true informal-formal pairs correctly identified by the model. It is calculated as: Recall\\=T​PT​P+F​N\\\\text{Recall}=\\\\frac{TP}{TP+FN}, where F​NFN represents the number of false negatives (missed aligned pairs).\n\nTable 3: Automated Alignment Evaluation (AAE) results across different benchmarks. The table compares the performance of our fine-tuned Mistral-7B model (FormalAlign model) with GPT-4 and GPT-3.5 on four datasets: FormL4-Basic, FormL4-Random, MiniF2F-Valid, and MiniF2F-Test. Performance metrics include Alignment Score (AS), Precision (Prec.), and Recall (Rec.).\n\nDatasets\n\nFormL4-Basic\n\nFormL4-Random\n\nMiniF2F-Valid\n\nMiniF2F-Test\n\nAS\n\nPrec.\n\nRec.\n\nAS\n\nPrec.\n\nRec.\n\nAS\n\nPrec.\n\nRec.\n\nAS\n\nPrec.\n\nRec.\n\nGPT-4\n\n88.91\n\n26.33\n\n88.69\n\n90.52\n\n28.56\n\n90.02\n\n64.34\n\n44.58\n\n90.98\n\n68.31\n\n51.11\n\n94.65\n\nGPT-3.5\n\n50.23\n\n25.21\n\n90.83\n\n47.00\n\n23.42\n\n67.26\n\n47.32\n\n22.29\n\n62.55\n\n40.74\n\n21.97\n\n61.73\n\nFormalAlign\n\n99.21\n\n93.65\n\n86.43\n\n85.85\n\n86.90\n\n89.20\n\n66.39\n\n68.58\n\n60.66\n\n64.61\n\n66.70\n\n63.37\n\n### 4.3 Main Results\n\nWe fine-tune a Mistral-7B model ([Jiang et al., 2023b](#bib.bib19)) as the FormalAlign model and evaluate its performance on various autoformalization benchmarks. The datasets used in this study include FormL4-Basic, FormL4-Random, MiniF2F-Valid, and MiniF2F-Test. Each data example consists of an aligned informal-formal pair, which is considered a positive example. To comprehensively assess the model’s performance and robustness, we augment each positive example with 21 negative examples generated through carefully designed misalignment strategies outlined in [Table 2](#S4.T2 \"In 4.1 Datasets ‣ 4 Experiment ‣ FormalAlign: Automated Alignment Evaluation for Autoformalization\").\n\nTo balance precision and recall in the FormalAlign model’s alignment detection, we set θ\\=0.7\\\\theta=0.7. [Table 3](#S4.T3 \"In 4.2 Metrics ‣ 4.1 Datasets ‣ 4 Experiment ‣ FormalAlign: Automated Alignment Evaluation for Autoformalization\") presents the detailed experimental results, including Alignment-Selection (AS), Precision (Prec.), and Recall (Rec.) metrics. The table compares the performance of our fine-tuned Mistral-7B model (FormalAlign model) with GPT-4 ([Achiam et al., 2023](#bib.bib1)) and GPT-3.5 ([OpenAI, 2023](#bib.bib35)) across the different datasets. For more information on the query prompts used in the experiments, please refer to [Section C.2](#A3.SS2 \"C.2 Prompt Details ‣ Appendix C Experimental Details ‣ 6 Conclusion ‣ 5.3 Effects of Different Alignment Score 𝒱_\\\"align\\\" ‣ 5 Analysis and Discussion ‣ 4.4 Comparison with Human Evaluation and LLM-As-Judge ‣ 4.3 Main Results ‣ 4.2 Metrics ‣ 4.1 Datasets ‣ 4 Experiment ‣ FormalAlign: Automated Alignment Evaluation for Autoformalization\").\n\nEffective and Robust Alignment Evaluation: The experimental results demonstrate the effectiveness and robustness of our FormalAlign in evaluating the alignment between informal and formal languages. The model achieves impressive performance, with high alignment, precision, and recall scores across all datasets. Notably, on the FormL4-Basic dataset, it attains an exceptional Alignment-Selection score of 99.21% and a Precision of 93.65%. These results highlight the model’s ability to accurately identify aligned informal-formal pairs.\n\nGeneralization Across Datasets: The FormalAlign model exhibits consistent performance across four diverse datasets, demonstrating its ability to generalize its autoformalization evaluation capabilities. Particularly noteworthy are the model’s AS scores of 66.39% and 64.61% on the challenging MiniF2F-Valid and MiniF2F-Test datasets, respectively. These scores are comparable to those achieved by GPT-4, which obtained AS scores of 64.34% and 68.31% on the same datasets. The FormalAlign model’s strong performance on the MiniF2F theorem proving benchmark, which poses significant challenges due to its complexity and diversity, highlights the effectiveness of our proposed FormalAlign in enhancing the model’s generalization ability.\n\nThe experimental results validate the effectiveness of FormalAlign in improving the performance of LLMs for autoformalization alignment evaluation. The integration of cross-entropy loss with contrastive learning in the model’s training process has proven to be a powerful combination, resulting in a robust model capable of achieving high alignment-selection, precision, and recall scores across various datasets. The model’s ability to generalize its performance to challenging benchmarks like MiniF2F further underscores the benefits of our approach.\n\n### 4.4 Comparison with Human Evaluation and LLM-As-Judge\n\nTo comprehensively assess our FormalAlign model in autoformalization alignment evaluation, we conduct an extensive human evaluation along with an LLM-as-judge evaluation and compared their correctness rates. This analysis offers an in-depth understanding of our automated evaluation method’s performance compared to human experts and state-of-the-art language models.\n\nThe experiment design, statistical results, as well as detailed discussions are specified in Appendix [G](#A7 \"Appendix G Comparison with Human Evaluation and LLM-as-judge ‣ 6 Conclusion ‣ 5.3 Effects of Different Alignment Score 𝒱_\\\"align\\\" ‣ 5 Analysis and Discussion ‣ 4.4 Comparison with Human Evaluation and LLM-As-Judge ‣ 4.3 Main Results ‣ 4.2 Metrics ‣ 4.1 Datasets ‣ 4 Experiment ‣ FormalAlign: Automated Alignment Evaluation for Autoformalization\"). As listed in [11](#A7.T11 \"Table 11 ‣ Results ‣ Appendix G Comparison with Human Evaluation and LLM-as-judge ‣ 6 Conclusion ‣ 5.3 Effects of Different Alignment Score 𝒱_\\\"align\\\" ‣ 5 Analysis and Discussion ‣ 4.4 Comparison with Human Evaluation and LLM-As-Judge ‣ 4.3 Main Results ‣ 4.2 Metrics ‣ 4.1 Datasets ‣ 4 Experiment ‣ FormalAlign: Automated Alignment Evaluation for Autoformalization\"), human experts achieved the highest correctness ratio in matching with the ground-truth alignment evaluations with an average of 79.58%, followed by our FormalAlign (65.00%). The LLM-as-judge method achieves the lowest precision in autoformalization alignment evaluation. Each human expert takes approximately 3 hours to review 80 items, while the FormalAlign model requires less than 2 minutes to conduct the automated evaluation. These findings emphasize the value of our FormalAlign framework in providing an efficient and reliable automated evaluation method for autoformalization alignment.\n\n## 5 Analysis and Discussion\n\nTo further validate the robustness and effectiveness of our FormalAlign framework, we conducted seven additional experiments, some of which are detailed in the Appendix due to limited space. We begin by validating the generalized effect of our FormalAlign across different baseline language models ([Section 5.1](#S5.SS1 \"5.1 Effects of Different Baselines ‣ 5 Analysis and Discussion ‣ 4.4 Comparison with Human Evaluation and LLM-As-Judge ‣ 4.3 Main Results ‣ 4.2 Metrics ‣ 4.1 Datasets ‣ 4 Experiment ‣ FormalAlign: Automated Alignment Evaluation for Autoformalization\")). We investigate the necessity and effectiveness of our combined training loss ([Section 5.2](#S5.SS2 \"5.2 Effects of Different Training Loss ‣ 5 Analysis and Discussion ‣ 4.4 Comparison with Human Evaluation and LLM-As-Judge ‣ 4.3 Main Results ‣ 4.2 Metrics ‣ 4.1 Datasets ‣ 4 Experiment ‣ FormalAlign: Automated Alignment Evaluation for Autoformalization\")) and the impact of our proposed alignment score 𝒱align\\\\mathcal{V}\\_{\\\\text{align}} ([Section 5.3](#S5.SS3 \"5.3 Effects of Different Alignment Score 𝒱_\\\"align\\\" ‣ 5 Analysis and Discussion ‣ 4.4 Comparison with Human Evaluation and LLM-As-Judge ‣ 4.3 Main Results ‣ 4.2 Metrics ‣ 4.1 Datasets ‣ 4 Experiment ‣ FormalAlign: Automated Alignment Evaluation for Autoformalization\")).\n\n\n...66512 bytes truncated...\n\nThe tool call succeeded but the output was truncated. Full output saved to: /home/hal/.local/share/opencode/tool-output/tool_14b697999001g67JLN2kNidq1r\nUse Grep to search the full content or Read with offset/limit to view specific sections.","metadata":{"truncated":true,"outputPath":"/home/hal/.local/share/opencode/tool-output/tool_14b697999001g67JLN2kNidq1r"},"title":"https://arxiv.org/html/2410.10135v1 (text/html; charset=utf-8)","time":{"start":1792266565850,"end":1792266566058}},"id":"prt_14b6978c9001xvl10rsWpPWHKV","sessionID":"ses_eb497bcceffeROWha5Uz0kLbec","messageID":"msg_14b686479001xsrRXuys1OirZH"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1792266566114,"sessionID":"ses_eb497bcceffeROWha5Uz0kLbec","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"webfetch","callID":"call_3rvv2k0aifsy41yrpcwxh5yc","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.22763v1"},"output":" \\[2605.22763v1\\] Advancing Mathematics Research with AI-Driven Formal Proof Search                               \n\n[Skip to main content](#content)\n\n[](https://arxiv.org/IgnoreMe)[![archive](/static/base/1.0.1/images/arxiv-logo-primary-light.svg)](https://arxiv.org/)\n\n[Search](https://arxiv.org/search) [Submit](https://arxiv.org/user/create) [Donate](https://info.arxiv.org/about/donate.html) [Log in](https://arxiv.org/login)\n\nSearch arXiv   \n\nPress Enter to search · [Advanced search](https://arxiv.org/search/advanced)\n\n# Computer Science > Artificial Intelligence\n\n**arXiv:2605.22763v1** (cs)\n\n\\[Submitted on 21 May 2026 (this version), *latest version 8 Jun 2026* ([v2](https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.22763v2))\\]\n\n# Title:Advancing Mathematics Research with AI-Driven Formal Proof Search\n\nAuthors:[George Tsoukalas](https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&query=Tsoukalas,+G), [Anton Kovsharov](https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&query=Kovsharov,+A), [Sergey Shirobokov](https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&query=Shirobokov,+S), [Anja Surina](https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&query=Surina,+A), [Moritz Firsching](https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&query=Firsching,+M), [Gergely Bérczi](https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&query=B%C3%A9rczi,+G), [Francisco J. R. Ruiz](https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&query=Ruiz,+F+J+R), [Arun Suggala](https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&query=Suggala,+A), [Adam Zsolt Wagner](https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&query=Wagner,+A+Z), [Eric Wieser](https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&query=Wieser,+E), [Lei Yu](https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&query=Yu,+L), [Aja Huang](https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&query=Huang,+A), [Miklós Z. Horváth](https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&query=Horv%C3%A1th,+M+Z), [Andrew Ferrauiolo](https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&query=Ferrauiolo,+A), [Henryk Michalewski](https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&query=Michalewski,+H), [Codrut Grosu](https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&query=Grosu,+C), [Thomas Hubert](https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&query=Hubert,+T), [Matej Balog](https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&query=Balog,+M), [Pushmeet Kohli](https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&query=Kohli,+P), [Swarat Chaudhuri](https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&query=Chaudhuri,+S)\n\nView a PDF of the paper titled Advancing Mathematics Research with AI-Driven Formal Proof Search, by George Tsoukalas and 19 other authors\n\n[View PDF](/pdf/2605.22763v1) [HTML (experimental)](https://arxiv.org/html/2605.22763v1)\n\n> Abstract:Large language models (LLMs) increasingly excel at mathematical reasoning, but their unreliability limits their utility in mathematics research. A mitigation is using LLMs to generate formal proofs in languages like Lean. We perform the first large-scale evaluation of this method's ability to solve open problems. Our most capable agent autonomously resolved 9 of 353 open Erdős problems at the per-problem cost of a few hundred dollars, proved 44/492 OEIS conjectures, and is being deployed in combinatorics, optimization, graph theory, algebraic geometry, and quantum optics research. A basic agent alternating LLM-based generation with Lean-based verification replicated the Erdős successes but proved costlier on the hardest problems. These findings demonstrate the power of AI-aided formal proof search and shed light on the agent designs that enable it.\n\nComments:\n\nThe first three authors and the last author have equal contributions. The first three authors are in random order\n\nSubjects:\n\n