{"type":"text","timestamp":1792266721156,"sessionID":"ses_eb497bcceffeROWha5Uz0kLbec","part":{"id":"prt_14b6bc78f001Y0XNeISmCVcbb5","messageID":"msg_14b6aa7bd001idS2o7M97kGKtQ","sessionID":"ses_eb497bcceffeROWha5Uz0kLbec","type":"text","text":"I have fetched all sources and verified each quote. Here is the result.\n\n```json\n{\"items\": [\n  {\"ref\": \"p1:c1\", \"status\": \"SUPPORTED\", \"page_quote\": \"Training completed in under 24 hours using 8 H100 GPUs. Each problem receives 16 total formalizations (8 from each formalizer).\", \"note\": \"Page also states '928K informal statements processed', '1.64M formalized statements' and 'Total: 1.78M formal statements' (with 140K Leanworkbook), matching every figure in the item.\"},\n  {\"ref\": \"p1:c2\", \"status\": \"SUPPORTED\", \"page_quote\": \"Uses Qwen2.5-72B-Instruct for assessment ... Generates 4 independent judgments per statement ... Statements below 0.5 FC score are filtered out\", \"note\": \"These bullets appear under the Faithfulness and Completeness (FC) Test on the project page; no human review or hidden-state probes are described for semantic checking.\"},\n  {\"ref\": \"p1:c3\", \"status\": \"SUPPORTED\", \"page_quote\": \"LLMs are used to check that the formal statements accurately preserve the content of the original natural language problems.\", \"note\": \"Verbatim in the paper abstract; Section 3.1 links this FC-test filtering to the 1.64M formal statements.\"},\n  {\"ref\": \"p1:done\", \"status\": \"SUPPORTED\", \"page_quote\": \"FC Test: Using LLM as judge to verify accuracy and completeness\", \"note\": \"Page lists only CC Test (Lean compiler) and FC Test (LLM judge) as quality gates; it describes no hidden-state probes or machine-vs-expert label comparison.\"},\n  {\"ref\": \"p2:c1\", \"status\": \"SUPPORTED\", \"page_quote\": \"We then employ a QwQ-32B as a judge to evaluate the semantic correctness of each remaining formalization, using a handcrafted prompt to guide its assessment.\", \"note\": \"Verbatim (Appendix C.2.2); the following sentence confirms multiple judge samples with unanimous voting, and the judge is external to the formalizer.\"},\n  {\"ref\": \"p2:c2\", \"status\": \"SUPPORTED\", \"page_quote\": \"we monitor model outputs throughout training with Lean 4 experts and gradually improve the judge prompt over iterations\", \"note\": \"Verbatim (Appendix C.2.2), in the context that LLM-based feedback is susceptible to false positives.\"},\n  {\"ref\": \"p2:c3\", \"status\": \"SUPPORTED\", \"page_quote\": \"We note that this method required careful expert monitoring as we observe that the model would repeatedly make mistakes that the LLM judge model cannot catch, as long as it passed compilation.\", \"note\": \"Verbatim. Page also states 'a human-curated test set of size approximately 1,000' and 'our annotated statements are limited in number—approximately 10k—compared to the 100k generated by autoformalization', matching the item.\"},\n  {\"ref\": \"p2:done\", \"status\": \"SUPPORTED\", \"page_quote\": \"Evaluated across four benchmarks augmented by our proposed misalignment strategies, FormalAlign demonstrates superior performance.\", \"note\": \"Verbatim in abstract. FormalAlign's HTML confirms 'We fine-tune a Mistral-7B model as the FormalAlign model' and training via 'contrastive loss in hidden states', i.e., a separately fine-tuned evaluator on synthetic misalignments, not linear probes; Kimina paper confirms production uses QwQ judge + experts. Authors Jing Xiong and Zhengying Liu are listed. Only the 'ICLR 2025' venue (and 'Jing Xiong as Kimina contributor') are not stated on these pages.\"},\n  {\"ref\": \"p3:c1\", \"status\": \"SUPPORTED\", \"page_quote\": \"We established a bridge between these two complementary spheres by fine-tuning a Gemini model to automatically translate natural language problem statements into formal statements, creating a large library of formal problems of varying difficulty.\", \"note\": \"Verbatim in the blog.\"},\n  {\"ref\": \"p3:c2\", \"status\": \"SUPPORTED\", \"page_quote\": \"Around one million informal math problems are translated into a formal math language by a formalizer network.\", \"note\": \"Verbatim (infographic caption) in the blog.\"},\n  {\"ref\": \"p3:c3\", \"status\": \"SUPPORTED\", \"page_quote\": \"First, the problems were manually translated into formal mathematical language for our systems to understand.\", \"note\": \"Verbatim in the blog, describing the contest procedure.\"},\n  {\"ref\": \"p3:c4\", \"status\": \"SUPPORTED\", \"page_quote\": \"We used Gemini to autoformalize 492 open questions from the OEIS and applied the agent to the resulting Lean statements.\", \"note\": \"Verbatim in the paper (OEIS section). Abstract states per-problem cost of 'a few hundred dollars' and AlphaProof ~$60/problem, supporting substantial per-problem compute; the paper treats misformalization as a risk requiring 'test lemmas' as guards.\"},\n  {\"ref\": \"p3:done\", \"status\": \"SUPPORTED\", \"page_quote\": \"First, the problems were manually translated into formal mathematical language for our systems to understand.\", \"note\": \"Verbatim. The blog describes only the fine-tuned Gemini formalizer (training) and manual translation (contest); it reports no probe-based misformalization detection or machine-vs-expert label experiment.\"}\n]}\n```","time":{"start":1792266717071,"end":1792266721131}}}
